In this lesson, we'll look at what happens when a single line crosses two parallel lines. That crossing line is called a transversal, and together they form eight angles — four at each intersection.

The first pair to know is corresponding angles. These sit in the same position at each intersection, like both top-left, and they're always equal.

Next, alternate angles. These sit on opposite sides of the transversal, between the two parallel lines, making a Z-shape. They're also equal.

Then we have co-interior angles, sometimes called allied or same-side angles. These sit on the same side of the transversal, between the parallel lines, forming a C or U shape. Instead of being equal, they add up to one hundred and eighty degrees — they're supplementary.

Why do these rules work? Imagine sliding one parallel line along the transversal until it sits right on top of the other. Corresponding angles land perfectly on each other, alternate angles match after a flip, and co-interior angles form a straight line, which is why they sum to one hundred and eighty.

Quick warning: these relationships only apply when the lines are actually parallel. If they aren't, the rules don't hold.

Now let's see it in action.